export const articles=[
{
slug:'addition-budget',title:'Planning a San Diego Home Addition: Budget, Structure & Schedule',description:'Build a complete addition plan around site work, foundations, roof connections, utilities, approvals and the sequence that keeps the existing home protected.',
intro:'A home addition has two jobs: create useful new space and connect it properly to the house you already own. The connection often explains why a simple square-foot estimate misses important work. Plan the old and new construction together before comparing prices.',
sections:[
['Define the room and its connection','<p>Draw how people will enter the addition, where furniture will sit and which existing room changes to make it work. A new bedroom may also require a hallway, relocated window or revised heating distribution. Include those changes in the scope rather than pricing only the new footprint.</p><p>Compare an addition with a rearrangement of existing space if both could solve the problem. Evaluate useful area, daylight and circulation—not just the amount of floor area added.</p>'],
['Investigate the foundation and site',`<p>Have the designer identify the survey, soil and existing-foundation information needed for the proposal. The old foundation’s capacity and the new work’s connection cannot be established by visual similarity alone. List required investigation before committing to a foundation assumption.</p><p>In the City of San Diego, the building-permit guidance directs homeowners to residential addition requirements. Grading and other approvals may also apply to the site. Start with the responsible jurisdiction, not a County-wide assumption. ${ref('additionSD')}.</p>`],
['Resolve the roof before promising an interior ceiling','<p>Show how roof geometry, runoff and weather protection connect at the existing house. Ask the designer to coordinate the exterior roof line with the ceiling height you expect indoors. A convenient roof connection can still produce an awkward low ceiling or drainage problem if those decisions are made separately.</p><p>Identify how the existing home will be protected while the opening is made. Temporary weather protection, repairs to the old roof and restoration of adjoining finishes belong in the estimate.</p>'],
['Build a complete budget rather than one rate','<ul><li>Design, investigation, engineering and authority fees.</li><li>Access, demolition, earthwork and foundation construction.</li><li>Framing, roofing, windows and exterior weather protection.</li><li>Electrical, plumbing and heating or cooling extensions.</li><li>Interior finishes and repairs within the existing house.</li><li>Site restoration and a reserve for unresolved conditions.</li></ul><p>Ask which items are priced, allowances or exclusions. Do not compare a shell-only addition price with a finished-room proposal.</p>'],
['Sequence the work around dependency points','<p>Track design and approvals, procurement, foundation work, framing, weather enclosure, rough systems, inspections and finishes. The exact order depends on the design, but the schedule should show what must be verified before concealment and when the old house becomes exposed.</p><p>If you will remain at home, identify the periods when access, privacy or utilities are affected. A smaller addition can still disrupt the existing kitchen or bathroom if its connection passes through that space.</p>']
],faq:[['Can an addition use the same foundation detail as a nearby house?','Do not assume so. Site conditions, loads and existing construction need project-specific evaluation.'],['Is a price per square foot enough to set the budget?','It can be an early reference only when its scope is clear. Separate the site work and existing-house repairs to understand what the figure leaves out.']],sources:['additionSD'],links:[['Structural opening decisions','structural-changes.html'],['Hillside and drainage planning','hillside-properties.html'],['Home addition services','../home-additions.html']]
},
{
slug:'bathroom-vanity-planning',title:'Bathroom Layout: Vanities, Storage, Lighting & Bathtubs',description:'Coordinate vanity size, storage, lighting, plumbing and tub access before ordering fixtures for a bathroom remodel.',
intro:'A bathroom can have attractive fixtures and still be frustrating to use. Plan the vanity, storage, lighting and bathing area as one room. The useful test is whether doors, drawers, people and maintenance access can coexist in the space available.',
sections:[
['Start with movement and storage needs','<p>Measure the room and draw door swings, drawers and the approach to each fixture. List what actually needs storage: towels, cleaning products, toiletries and electrical grooming items. Decide what should be reachable daily and what can live elsewhere.</p><p>A wider vanity can reduce circulation without adding much useful storage if plumbing occupies its center. Compare drawer arrangements and access around the sink before treating overall width as the only measure of value.</p>'],
['Coordinate the sink and plumbing before cabinet approval','<p>Choose the sink type and faucet arrangement with the vanity drawing. Check the sink bowl, drain, supply connections and drawer boxes together. A wall-mounted faucet requires coordination inside the wall; it cannot be treated as a finish selection to settle after tile.</p><p>If fixtures move, have the plumber and designer establish the route and restoration work. Keep service access and shutoffs usable. Include countertop holes and accessory locations in the approved package.</p>'],
['Use light where the face and floor need it',`<p>Evaluate mirror lighting with the user standing at the sink, and include general light and nighttime movement. Avoid choosing fixtures solely from their appearance above an empty vanity. Confirm suitability for the proposed wet or damp location and compatibility with controls.</p><p>California’s lighting guidance covers alterations as well as new work. Have the electrician confirm the requirements for the chosen fixtures and controls. ${ref('lightsCEC')}.</p>`],
['Choose a tub by access and maintenance as well as shape','<p>Compare alcove, freestanding and other tub arrangements using the actual product drawings. Check the space to get in and out, the reach to controls, the route for installation and access for service. A freestanding tub placed too close to a wall can leave an awkward cleaning gap.</p><p>Have the project team evaluate support, water supply and drainage needs for the selected tub. Consider whether the household will use it often enough to justify the floor area compared with a larger shower or more storage.</p>'],
['Approve one coordinated bathroom drawing','<ul><li>Vanity, sink and faucet specifications with plumbing clearances.</li><li>Drawer and door operation, including adjacent room doors.</li><li>Mirror, fixture and control locations.</li><li>Tub installation requirements and service access.</li><li>Storage that does not interfere with waterproofing or structure.</li></ul><p>Use a full-size mockup or taped layout where space is tight. Resolve the conflicts before ordering rather than asking the installer to make the products fit after delivery.</p>']
],faq:[['Are two sinks always better than one?','Not if they remove the counter and storage your household needs. Compare how two people use the room at the same time with the space each arrangement leaves.'],['Can I recess a medicine cabinet wherever I want?','Have the wall checked for structure, wiring and plumbing first. A recess needs a suitable location and coordination with the mirror and lighting.']],sources:['lightsCEC'],links:[['Accessible bathroom planning','accessible-bathroom-design.html'],['Plumbing and fixture moves','whole-home-plumbing.html'],['Bathroom remodel costs','../bathroom-remodel-cost-san-diego.html']]
},
{
slug:'bathroom-tile-layout',title:'Choosing and Installing Tile: Layout, Grout & Wet-Area Details',description:'Plan tile for bathrooms, floors and exterior areas using product suitability, substrate preparation, layout drawings, movement joints and care requirements.',
intro:'Tile selection is only one part of a durable installation. The surface underneath, water-management details, movement joints and layout all affect the result. Settle those decisions before comparing tile-setting prices or ordering the full quantity.',
sections:[
['Match the tile to its intended location',`<p>Porcelain is a category of ceramic tile defined by low water absorption; the name alone does not establish suitability for every floor or outdoor condition. TCNA advises checking manufacturer information for the intended use. ${ref('tileType')}.</p><p>Ask for the product’s floor, wall and exterior application guidance, including wet-use information where relevant. Evaluate texture and cleaning with a sample. No tile label removes the need to manage water, slopes and maintenance.</p>`],
['Approve the layout where cuts will be visible','<p>Draw the major joints, focal walls, niches, drains and transitions. Check the pattern at doorways and around fixtures rather than centering each wall independently. A tile that looks balanced on one wall can create a narrow cut at the next corner.</p><p>Discuss variation and blending before installation. Keep samples or photos of the agreed arrangement, including grout color and joint appearance. Confirm the order quantity with the installer after the pattern and cuts are established.</p>'],
['Price the preparation and wet-area assembly','<p>Ask the installer to assess the substrate and specify the preparation needed for the chosen tile. Large pieces make surface irregularities especially important to resolve. Include removal, repair and transitions in the quote rather than assuming the surface will be ready.</p><p>In a shower, the waterproofing and drain connection require their own assembly and verification. A tile-setting price or a grout sealer does not define that work. Keep the inspection and testing sequence ahead of concealment.</p>'],
['Plan grout and movement joints separately',`<p>Grout fills the tile joints; it does not replace the flexible accommodation needed for movement. TCNA explains that movement-joint placement depends on site conditions, including sunlight and moisture exposure. Ask the installer to identify the layout and appropriate materials. ${ref('tileMovement')}.</p><p>Select grout compatible with the tile and joint design, using the product instructions for installation and cure. Ask whether the specified grout needs sealing and how it should be cleaned. ${ref('tileGrout')}.</p>`],
['Treat outdoor tile as a distinct assembly','<p>For a patio or exterior landing, evaluate drainage, exposure, substrate and transitions at the building. Do not carry an interior tile specification outdoors just to maintain the same appearance. Ask how water reaches an approved discharge point and how movement is accommodated.</p><p>Compare the finished threshold height and access needs with the drainage design. A visually flush transition should not be achieved by ignoring weather protection at the door.</p>'],
['Keep installation and care records','<p>Retain product names, grout and sealant information, spare material and approved cleaning instructions. Observe the specified cure and return-to-use times. If joints crack or tiles become loose, have the condition assessed before covering the symptoms with new grout.</p>']
],faq:[['Is porcelain automatically the best tile for a shower floor?','Not necessarily. Check the exact product’s intended use, texture, dimensions and compatibility with the drain and floor slopes.'],['Can I eliminate grout joints for a seamless look?','Do not assume that a tight visual joint can replace the installation requirements. Have the installer establish appropriate joints and movement accommodation for the chosen product.']],sources:['tileType','tileMovement','tileGrout'],links:[['Shower waterproofing','shower-waterproofing.html'],['Large porcelain slabs','large-format-porcelain-slabs.html'],['Tile and stone services','../tile-stone-installation.html']]
},
{
slug:'marble-countertops',title:'Marble Countertops: Etching, Staining & the Choice to Accept Patina',description:'Decide whether marble fits your kitchen by understanding appearance changes, slab layout, sealing limits and the care you are prepared to provide.',
intro:'Marble is a decision about how you want a surface to age as much as how you want it to look on installation day. If every dull spot would feel like damage, discuss that expectation before ordering. A protective treatment does not turn marble into a maintenance-free surface.',
sections:[
['Distinguish a stain from an etched finish',`<p>The Natural Stone Institute warns that acidic products can dull or etch calcareous stone. It also explains that sealing improves stain resistance without making stone stain-proof. These are different issues: a sealer should not be presented as a promise against all surface changes. ${ref('stoneCare')}.</p><p>Ask the supplier to explain the particular marble and finish you are considering. View examples that have been used, not only polished showroom samples. Decide whether visible wear is acceptable in the area where you plan to install it.</p>`],
['Choose where the material earns its place','<p>Compare using marble throughout the kitchen with using it on a limited surface. Consider where acidic foods, spills, heat and heavy daily use are most likely. The right answer depends on the household’s habits and tolerance for maintenance, not a claim that marble is either always impractical or always worth it.</p><p>If a different material will be used nearby, review the samples together. Coordinate thickness, edge profiles and transitions so the combination looks intentional.</p>'],
['Approve slabs and fabrication details','<p>See the actual slabs where possible and discuss natural variation, repairs or filled features with the fabricator. Review vein direction at seams, sink openings and returns. Record the chosen layout before cutting.</p><p>Confirm support requirements, edge details, delivery access and the condition of the cabinets. A dramatic overhang or waterfall edge needs an installation plan, not just an inspiration photograph.</p>'],
['Agree on care and repair expectations','<p>Obtain the stone-specific cleaning and sealing recommendation. Ask which products are suitable, what a maintenance visit might involve and who evaluates a mark that cannot be removed by ordinary cleaning. Keep abrasive or acidic household remedies off the surface unless the supplier confirms their suitability.</p><p>Discuss the difference between maintaining the chosen finish and restoring it. Do not assume a spot repair will always disappear under every lighting condition.</p>'],
['Compare the full decision','<p>Include fabrication, installation, support, plumbing work and future care in your evaluation. A sample you love but are afraid to use may be a poor fit for the main cooking surface.</p><p>Before ordering, describe your expectations plainly: whether you want a continually uniform finish or can accept gradual changes. That conversation is more useful than a broad durability ranking.</p>']
],faq:[['Will sealing stop lemon juice from marking marble?','Do not rely on that. Acid-related etching and absorption-related staining are different concerns; ask the stone supplier what protection and maintenance are realistic.'],['Is a honed finish damage-proof?','No. A different finish can change the appearance of wear, but it does not remove the material’s care requirements.']],sources:['stoneCare'],links:[['Granite selection and care','granite-countertops.html'],['Quartz selection and care','quartz-countertops.html'],['Bookmatched stone planning','bookmatched-stone.html']]
},
{
slug:'butcher-block-countertops',title:'Wood Countertops: Finish, Sink Details & Maintenance',description:'Plan a wood countertop around its finish system, intended use, moisture exposure, movement and care before choosing the species or edge.',
intro:'The finish on a wood countertop changes how you use and maintain it. Decide whether the surface is intended for direct food preparation, general counter use or a decorative accent before choosing the product. A cutting board routine is not automatically the right routine for every finished wood counter.',
sections:[
['Choose the use and finish together',`<p>John Boos publishes separate care guidance for oil-finished and Varnique-finished countertops and worktops. That distinction is a useful reminder to identify the actual finish before selecting cleaners or treatments. Follow the instructions for the product you buy, rather than applying one brand’s routine to all wood. ${ref('woodTop')}.</p><p>Ask whether direct cutting is intended, how heat should be managed and what contact with water the installation is designed to accommodate. Keep the written answers with the order.</p>`],
['Review water exposure at the sink','<p>A sink opening, faucet hole and wall junction all need coordination with the selected countertop system. Ask the fabricator to specify the cutout treatment and explain how exposed edges and joints will be protected. Include the faucet and sink model before fabrication.</p><p>Consider whether the household is willing to manage standing water and keep the finish maintained. If the sink is heavily used, compare a different surface in that area with wood used elsewhere in the kitchen.</p>'],
['Allow for the material and its installation','<p>Have the fabricator identify support, attachment and movement requirements for the chosen construction. Do not assume a solid-looking top can be fastened like a stone slab. Coordinate adjoining walls, appliances and changes of material with the installer.</p><p>Confirm the supplied dimensions, edge treatment and finish on all relevant surfaces. If field cutting is needed, establish who restores the specified finish before the counter goes into use.</p>'],
['Keep maintenance practical','<p>Request a written routine that names the approved products and the signs that attention is needed. Keep that routine separate from ordinary cleaning. Discuss what a scratch, dry area or water mark should prompt you to do and who can assess a problem.</p><p>A heavily used kitchen needs a routine the household will actually follow. If ongoing finish care is unwelcome, compare other countertop options before choosing wood solely for its initial appearance.</p>'],
['Inspect the completed installation','<ul><li>Confirm the product and finish match the order.</li><li>Check joints, cutouts and accessible edges with the installer.</li><li>Verify that the surface is ready for use under its finish instructions.</li><li>Collect care products or specifications and warranty terms.</li><li>Record the contact for repairs or refinishing.</li></ul><p>Keep a separate cutting board available unless the selected surface is specifically intended and maintained for direct cutting.</p>']
],faq:[['Should every wood countertop be oiled?','No. Identify the finish first. A film-finished countertop and an oil-finished work surface may require different treatment.'],['Can wood work next to a sink?','Evaluate the selected product, cutout details and maintenance expectations with the fabricator. Do not assume that any unfinished edge or ordinary furniture finish is suitable.']],sources:['woodTop'],links:[['Compare countertop materials','../material-selection-guide.html'],['Quartz countertops','quartz-countertops.html'],['Kitchen layout planning','kitchen-layout-design.html']]
},
{
slug:'coastal-home-remodeling',title:'Coastal San Diego Remodeling: Exposure, Materials & Approvals',description:'Plan coastal remodeling around actual salt and moisture exposure, compatible exterior components, maintenance access and location-specific approvals.',
intro:'“Near the coast” is not a complete material specification. A sheltered wall and an exposed deck on the same property can face different conditions. Describe the exposure at each part of the home, then select the assembly and maintenance plan that fit it.',
sections:[
['Map exposure rather than relying on the ZIP code','<p>Walk the property and note wind-driven rain, salt deposits, shaded areas, sun exposure and surfaces that stay wet. Photograph existing corrosion, staining or recurring finish failure for the project team to assess. Avoid assuming every mark has the same cause.</p><p>Use those observations when discussing windows, railings, cladding and exterior fixtures. Ask suppliers to identify the approved use and care requirements for the exact product in the proposed location.</p>'],
['Coordinate metals and fasteners',`<p>Simpson Strong-Tie’s corrosion guidance discusses environmental conditions, treated wood and compatibility between fasteners and connector hardware. A corrosion-resistant connector does not establish that an arbitrary fastener is suitable with it. Have the designer or installer specify the complete connection. ${ref('corrosion')}.</p><p>Request the coating or material specification in the quote rather than accepting “outdoor hardware” as the entire description. Where products meet, ask who confirms compatibility and warranty conditions.</p>`],
['Keep water management behind the finish','<p>Discuss drainage, flashing, openings and transitions before choosing the visible cladding. A durable finish cannot compensate for an unresolved junction at a window or deck. Identify where the assembly sheds water and how it can be inspected or maintained.</p><p>When replacing one component, ask how it connects to the existing weather protection. Include removal and restoration needed to make that connection rather than pricing only the visible replacement.</p>'],
['Separate exposure questions from coastal approvals',`<p>Material suitability and permitting are different checks. For a City of San Diego property, identify the building and planning approvals relevant to the scope and location through ${ref('additionSD')}. Other coastal cities have their own review processes.</p><p>Show changes to openings, decks, roof lines and exterior appearance accurately. Do not assume that using the same footprint resolves every approval question, or that a product marketed for coastal use has any permitting significance.</p>`],
['Design for the maintenance you can reach','<p>Ask how exterior glass, drains, metalwork and sealant joints will be inspected and serviced. A detail that requires difficult access for routine attention can create an ongoing burden. Include access and replacement options in the design discussion.</p><p>At handover, retain the product schedule and exposure-related care instructions. Report changes in corrosion, leakage or operation early, and have the underlying condition evaluated before simply repainting it.</p>']
],faq:[['Does stainless steel mean no maintenance?','No blanket claim is appropriate. The grade, environment, connection and manufacturer’s guidance matter. Ask for the specification and care instructions.'],['Does every coastal remodel need the same approval?','No. Jurisdiction, parcel location and the actual scope determine the review. Confirm those details before setting the construction schedule.']],sources:['corrosion','additionSD'],links:[['Service-area planning guides','../areas.html'],['Outdoor living planning','outdoor-kitchen-planning.html'],['Hillside and drainage questions','hillside-properties.html']]
},
{
slug:'mechanical-systems',title:'Home Comfort During a Remodel: HVAC, Windows & Insulation',description:'Coordinate heating, cooling, shade, windows, insulation and wall closure around actual room conditions and applicable California energy requirements.',
intro:'A room that feels hot or cold needs a diagnosis before it needs a larger HVAC unit. A remodel changes walls, windows, air movement and sometimes the size of the home. Plan those changes together so the equipment and the building support the same comfort goals.',
sections:[
['Describe the comfort problem precisely','<p>Record which rooms are uncomfortable, at what time of day and under which conditions. Note direct sun, closed doors, drafts and changes after previous remodeling. Give that information to the HVAC designer or contractor alongside the proposed plan.</p><p>Compare shade, window changes, insulation and distribution improvements before assuming equipment replacement is the only solution. The right combination depends on the building and how it is used.</p>'],
['Size and route the system for the revised home',`<p>Have a qualified professional evaluate the proposed loads and distribution, including additions and changed room boundaries. California’s energy guidance applies to specified additions and alterations, with requirements that depend on the work. ${ref('hvacCEC')}.</p><p>Resolve equipment and duct locations with framing, ceiling heights and service access. A new soffit or inaccessible filter should not be a surprise after the architectural design is approved.</p>`],
['Treat windows, shade and insulation as connected choices',`<p>Review the exposure of each window and how the proposed product affects light, views and comfort. Ask the designer to identify the applicable energy documentation rather than choosing solely by an advertised efficiency label. The CEC compliance manual includes chapters addressing the building envelope and alterations. ${ref('energyCEC')}.</p><p>Coordinate shade structures and exterior changes with local approvals. An interior blind may help with glare, but the design discussion should distinguish glare control from the overall heating and cooling strategy.</p>`],
['Set a wall-closure checkpoint','<p>Before insulation and drywall conceal the work, confirm required inspections, system testing, backing, access panels and approved revisions. Photograph useful concealed locations and retain the records.</p><p>Specify the drywall finish appropriate to the lighting and final appearance. Strong grazing light can expose surface variation that ordinary room lighting hides. Discuss acoustic goals separately; one added layer or product does not by itself establish the performance of a complete wall.</p>'],
['Verify operation and explain the controls','<p>Ask for commissioning or testing records required by the project, equipment manuals and a demonstration of controls and maintenance access. Confirm how ventilation functions alongside heating and cooling rather than assuming they are the same task.</p><p>Track unresolved comfort concerns after occupancy and give the contractor specific observations. Changing settings repeatedly without documenting conditions makes a persistent problem harder to diagnose.</p>']
],faq:[['Will a bigger system fix a hot room?','Not necessarily. The issue may involve solar exposure, distribution, controls or the building envelope. Have the cause evaluated before selecting equipment.'],['Does a small remodel trigger every energy upgrade?','No universal answer applies. Ask the project professional to identify the requirements for the actual scope, code edition and building type.']],sources:['hvacCEC','energyCEC'],links:[['Kitchen ventilation','kitchen-ventilation.html'],['Bathroom ventilation','bathroom-ventilation.html'],['Electrical capacity planning','whole-home-electrical.html']]
},
{
slug:'whole-home-electrical',title:'Electrical Planning for a Remodel: Loads, Layout & Service Work',description:'Plan electrical work around actual appliances, service capacity, circuit and lighting layouts, utility coordination and inspections before walls close.',
intro:'An electrical plan starts with what the remodeled home will need to power. New appliances, an addition or future equipment can change that picture. Have a qualified electrician evaluate the existing system and proposed loads before assuming a panel replacement is either necessary or sufficient.',
sections:[
['Make an equipment list before discussing capacity',`<p>List existing and proposed appliances and equipment with model information where available. Include planned future uses rather than hiding them in a vague request for spare capacity. SDG&E’s service guidance asks customers to work with an electrician or plumber on existing and new appliance loads. ${ref('utilitySD')}.</p><p>A count of empty breaker spaces is not a load calculation. Ask the electrician to explain the assessment and identify any information still needed before pricing a service change.</p>`],
['Coordinate utility work with construction','<p>If the project changes service needs or equipment location, establish who handles the utility application and who performs the private-side work. Identify any trenching, access, restoration and inspection dependencies.</p><p>Do not tie a guaranteed demolition date to an unconfirmed utility schedule. Keep utility scope and charges visible in the project budget, including any assumptions that have not yet been resolved.</p>'],
['Place electrical features on the actual room plan','<p>Review appliance connections, receptacles, switches and lighting against cabinets, furniture and door swings. Think through where the household charges devices or uses countertop appliances. Have the electrician apply the relevant requirements to the agreed arrangement.</p><p>Check controls from the entry routes and intended use positions. Confirm fixture and dimmer compatibility before purchasing a full set of products.</p>'],
['Use rough work as a verification point',`<p>Before walls close, review the locations against the current drawings and confirm required testing and inspections. City of San Diego permit guidance covers electrical work as part of its mechanical, electrical and plumbing services; verify the route for the whole remodel. ${ref('mepSD')}.</p><p>Do not ask a finish installer to conceal an unresolved inspection item. If the layout changes, document the affected work and determine whether the approved plans need revision.</p>`],
['Collect a usable handover','<ul><li>Required approval and inspection records.</li><li>A clear circuit directory and equipment information.</li><li>Control instructions and maintenance access.</li><li>Documentation of planned future capacity or provisions.</li><li>The contact for faults or follow-up work.</li></ul><p>This is planning guidance, not wiring instructions. Leave installation and diagnosis of electrical hazards to qualified professionals.</p>']
],faq:[['Does adding an electric appliance always require a service upgrade?','No. The electrician and utility need to evaluate the existing service and proposed loads. Do not infer the answer from the appliance alone.'],['Can outlet and switch locations wait until drywall?','That can create rework. Review them against the layout before rough work and confirm them before the walls are closed.']],sources:['utilitySD','mepSD'],links:[['Lighting layout','kitchen-lighting-plan.html'],['HVAC and comfort planning','mechanical-systems.html'],['Electrical remodeling services','../electrical-remodeling.html']]
},
{
slug:'whole-home-plumbing',title:'Remodel Plumbing: Fixture Moves, Existing Pipes & Water Use',description:'Coordinate drains, vents, supply lines, fixture compatibility and water-efficient selections before approving plumbing changes in a remodel.',
intro:'Moving a fixture changes more than the visible water connection. Drains, venting, access and surrounding repairs can affect the route and cost. Have the plumbing scope evaluated alongside the room plan before ordering fixtures or assuming the shortest line on a drawing is buildable.',
sections:[
['Separate the systems you are changing','<p>Ask the plumber to identify supply, drainage, venting and hot-water changes separately. Describe whether the project keeps fixtures in place, relocates them or adds new demand. Record which existing components have been evaluated and which remain concealed.</p><p>For a bathroom above another room or a condo, establish access and restoration responsibilities. Moving a drain may affect a ceiling or shared system outside the visible bathroom.</p>'],
['Choose fixtures with their hidden components','<p>Confirm rough-in dimensions, valve requirements, sink depth, faucet reach and service parts before approving the layout. A trim set that looks right may not fit an existing valve. Coordinate wall-mounted fixtures and concealed equipment before closing the wall.</p><p>Review drawer clearances, shutoff access and the route for replacement or servicing. Include installation and connection costs when comparing an owner-purchased fixture with a contractor-supplied one.</p>'],
['Evaluate existing piping without blanket assumptions','<p>Age, visible condition and the proposed alteration all matter. Ask what investigation supports retaining or replacing a component. Do not accept either “replace everything” or “it still works” without an explanation tied to the project.</p><p>Price necessary openings and restoration alongside the pipe work. If investigation changes the scope, document the finding and revised work before allowing it to disappear behind new finishes.</p>'],
['Use water-efficiency labels accurately',`<p>EPA’s WaterSense label covers categories including bathroom sink faucets, showerheads and toilets; it does not apply to every water-using product, including kitchen faucets. Check the exact model and category rather than using the label as a general claim about the whole remodel. ${ref('waterLabel')}.</p><p>Discuss hot-water delivery and leak prevention as well as fixture selection. Lower-flow products alone do not resolve a long wait for hot water or a leaking system. Avoid savings promises without a clear usage basis.</p>`],
['Plan testing, inspections and handover',`<p>Confirm permit responsibilities and required inspections before work is covered. The City of San Diego’s ${ref('mepSD')} is an entry point for its trade permits; other jurisdictions use their own process.</p><p>At completion, have the installer demonstrate shutoffs and operation, identify service access and provide product and approval records. Keep a written list of unresolved issues rather than treating a finished tile wall as proof that the plumbing is complete.</p>`]
],faq:[['Is keeping the same fixture location always cheaper?','It can reduce routing work, but existing damage, incompatible components or required corrections may still affect the scope. Compare complete proposals.'],['Does a WaterSense product guarantee a particular bill reduction?','No. Household use, existing fixtures and other water consumption affect the result. The label addresses qualifying product performance and efficiency.']],sources:['waterLabel','mepSD'],links:[['Bathroom layout coordination','bathroom-vanity-planning.html'],['Permit and inspection sequence','plan-review.html'],['Plumbing remodeling services','../plumbing-remodeling.html']]
}
,{
slug:'kitchen-lighting-plan',title:'Planning Kitchen and Outdoor Lighting That Works After Dark',description:'Plan task lighting, fixture placement, dimming and outdoor zones before electrical work. Use a room-by-room schedule to avoid shadows and glare.',
intro:'A ceiling full of lights can still leave the cutting board in shadow. Start with the surfaces you use, then decide where the light should come from. Decorative fixtures work better when they are part of that plan.',
sections:[
['Draw the work surfaces first','<p>Mark the sink, preparation counter, range, island seating and main walking routes on the cabinet plan. Think through an ordinary evening: unloading groceries, washing dishes and leaving a low light on after dinner. Each activity may need a different lighting setting.</p><p>Coordinate ceiling fixtures with cabinet fronts and the position of the person working. Under-cabinet lighting can help illuminate a counter, but its location and diffuser affect reflections on polished stone. Review an actual fixture sample where possible.</p>'],
['Keep a fixture and control schedule','<p>For each light, record its model, location, finish, light output, color temperature, dimmer compatibility and who supplies it. Check replacement access and the appearance of nearby fixtures together. A warm pendant and a much cooler counter light can make the same cabinet finish look inconsistent.</p><p>Group controls around use: preparation lights, dining or island lights, and general circulation. Ask the electrician to confirm the proposed combinations before ordering. Dimmable on a product box does not confirm compatibility with every dimmer.</p>'],
['Coordinate the installation before walls close',`<p>Locate drivers, wiring routes and accessible service points before cabinetry and drywall are finished. Confirm mounting support for the selected fixtures and identify who patches openings. California lighting and control requirements depend on the work; use the ${ref('lightsCEC')} with the project professional.</p><p>Walk the marked layout with the cabinet drawings in hand. An island moved during design can leave a previously centered junction box in the wrong place.</p>`],
['Treat exterior lighting as its own set of zones','<p>Outside, separate the entry, steps, dining area and landscape accents. Begin with safe circulation and useful light, then add decorative accents. Aim fixtures to limit glare into seating areas and neighboring windows; consider how planting will grow around them.</p><p>Select equipment for its actual outdoor exposure and have the electrician define supply, protection and controls. Do not extend an indoor fixture schedule into a wet location by assumption. Keep access to drivers and connections practical for maintenance.</p>'],
['Review the result at night','<p>At handover, try each control and dimming range. Look for reflections from a seated position, shadows on the working counter and glare along outdoor paths. Record any adjustments before the final punch list closes, and save the fixture schedule for future replacements.</p>']
],faq:[['Should all the lights have the same color temperature?','A consistent starting point often helps connected rooms, but evaluate the actual products and finishes together. Deliberate differences should be tested rather than discovered after installation.'],['Can lighting be decided after cabinets are installed?','Some selections can change later, but wiring, service access and integrated cabinet lights are easier to coordinate before installation.']],sources:['lightsCEC'],links:[['Kitchen layout decisions','kitchen-layout-design.html'],['Electrical upgrade planning','whole-home-electrical.html'],['Outdoor project planning','outdoor-kitchen-planning.html']]
},
{
slug:'outdoor-kitchen-planning',title:'Planning an Outdoor Kitchen, Patio and Deck in San Diego',description:'Define outdoor cooking, seating, utilities, drainage and shade together. Compare a complete scope and sequence before choosing appliances or deck finishes.',
intro:'The grill is usually the easiest part to picture. The harder questions are where people will sit, how utilities reach the space, where rainwater goes and what the project needs before a finished surface can be installed.',
sections:[
['Start with a usable plan, including the route from the house','<p>Draw the cooking area, food preparation surface, dining furniture and doors at the same scale. Leave room to carry a tray without walking through the cooking position. Mark existing trees, drains and equipment that still need access.</p><p>A compact cooking station near the kitchen may serve you better than a larger island at the far end of the yard. Decide whether the project is mainly for everyday meals, occasional entertaining or both.</p>'],
['Separate shade from cooking safety',`<p>Do not assume a patio cover makes an acceptable location for a grill. The ${ref('grillSafety')} warns against using grills indoors or under combustible surfaces. Have the selected appliance instructions and proposed location reviewed before designing an enclosure or roof around it.</p><p>Plan the cover’s attachment or supports, roof drainage and its effect on the house separately. Confirm property-specific permit requirements with the responsible city or county; the ${ref('permitDirectory')} is a starting point within the City of San Diego.</p>`],
['Price the hidden work alongside the visible finishes','<p>Break the budget into investigation and design, approvals, demolition, excavation, utilities, drainage, supporting structure, surfaces, appliances and restoration. Show the route and extent of each utility on the plan, including any work inside the house.</p><p>For example, a sink may add routing and connection work beyond the fixture itself. Ask for a comparison with and without that feature before treating it as a small accessory. Keep uncertain existing conditions visible as allowances or separately defined investigation.</p>'],
['Choose deck and patio materials for the actual setting','<p>Compare products for outdoor exposure, wet use, maintenance, repair and the feel of the sample in sun. Ask how the chosen system handles edges, transitions and movement. A deck board is a surface choice; its supporting structure, connections and any required guards still need their own design.</p><p>Request the exact manufacturer’s care and installation documents. Do not assume every composite, wood species or tile performs alike, or that a new surface will correct an unsound supporting assembly.</p>'],
['Sequence utilities and drainage before finished surfaces','<p>Settle approvals and the layout first. Then coordinate demolition, ground work, supports, underground services and required inspections before closing trenches or installing paving. Confirm appliance dimensions before building fixed openings.</p><p>Finish with equipment commissioning, control demonstrations and care records. Photograph concealed routes while they are accessible and keep those records with the final plan.</p>']
],faq:[['Can I build the patio now and add a kitchen later?','Yes, if the future layout and service routes are considered now. Otherwise the later work may require removing new paving or relocating drainage.'],['Does an outdoor kitchen need a permit?','The answer depends on its structures, utilities, location and jurisdiction. Submit the complete proposed scope for confirmation before construction.']],sources:['grillSafety','permitDirectory'],links:[['Site drainage and hillside planning','hillside-properties.html'],['Outdoor lighting zones','kitchen-lighting-plan.html'],['Outdoor living services','../pools-outdoor-living.html']]
},
{
slug:'pool-remodel-planning',title:'Planning a Pool Remodel: Scope, Equipment and Site Access',description:'Separate pool finish repairs from structural and equipment changes. Plan approvals, access, barriers, surrounding surfaces and startup responsibilities.',
intro:'A pool remodel can mean replacing a worn finish, changing equipment or rebuilding part of the pool. Those are different projects. A useful proposal explains what is changing, what has been assessed and what the surrounding yard needs afterward.',
sections:[
['Define the problem before selecting a finish','<p>List the concerns separately: rough surfaces, damaged tile, suspected leakage, unreliable equipment or an outdated layout. Ask what inspection supports the proposed remedy. New plaster should not be presented as a cure for an unexplained leak or movement.</p><p>Photograph visible conditions and gather available construction, repair and equipment records. Agree on how additional findings will be documented and priced if they become visible during the work.</p>'],
['Distinguish cosmetic, structural and equipment work','<p>A finish proposal should specify preparation, the replacement system, coping and tile transitions, and protection of items being retained. A change in shape, depth or built-in features needs a different level of design and review.</p><p>For equipment changes, identify the selected models, service access, controls and connections. Have qualified professionals evaluate electrical bonding and other safety systems affected by the work. Avoid treating those systems as optional accessories to the visible finish.</p>'],
['Confirm the current approval requirements',`<p>The City of San Diego’s ${ref('poolSD')} provides an application starting point. Because the bulletin includes older code references, confirm the current permit, barrier, equipment and anti-entrapment requirements with Development Services for your proposed scope. Other San Diego County jurisdictions have their own process.</p><p>Include surrounding fences, gates and access in that discussion. Agree on temporary separation of the work area from children, pets and visitors throughout construction.</p>`],
['Include the yard in the construction plan','<p>Map equipment access, demolition removal, material staging and the surfaces that will need restoration. A narrow side yard can change how work is carried out. Clarify whether replacement paving, landscaping and drainage corrections are included.</p><p>Have the contractor establish an approved approach to draining and managing water. Do not assume it can be discharged into the street or that emptying the pool is harmless under all site conditions.</p>'],
['Assign startup and handover responsibilities','<p>Before signing, identify who handles initial startup, equipment programming and the transition to ongoing pool service. Obtain written finish-care instructions, warranty terms and any restrictions during curing or startup.</p><p>At handover, request a demonstration, inspection records where applicable and an itemized list of remaining work. Keep the finish and equipment model information together so future service does not depend on guessing what was installed.</p>']
],faq:[['Can I choose the pool finish before the inspection?','You can explore options, but commit after the condition assessment and scope are clear. Preparation or repair needs can affect the final system and budget.'],['Should equipment replacement be bundled with resurfacing?','Evaluate the equipment on its condition and compatibility with the project. Combining work can be convenient, but replacement needs a stated reason.']],sources:['poolSD'],links:[['Outdoor project budgeting','outdoor-kitchen-planning.html'],['Documenting changes','payment-schedules.html'],['Project closeout records','whole-home-closeout.html']]
},
{
slug:'hillside-properties',title:'Remodeling a San Diego Hillside Home: Drainage, Access and Ground Work',description:'Plan slope-sensitive remodeling around site investigation, drainage, access, grading review and retaining structures before fixing an addition or outdoor layout.',
intro:'On a hillside property, the work outside the room can determine what is practical inside it. An addition, deck or new opening may involve foundations, retaining structures, equipment access and drainage that a floor plan alone cannot show.',
sections:[
['Start with the site information you already have','<p>Collect surveys, previous soils or geotechnical reports, drainage plans and records for retaining walls or additions. Mark visible cracks, recurring wet areas and places where runoff collects. These observations help define an investigation; they do not establish the cause.</p><p>Ask the design team which existing records are usable and what new evaluation the proposed work needs. A neighboring property’s solution is not a substitute for understanding your own site.</p>'],
['Check land-use and grading constraints early',`<p>The City of San Diego’s ${ref('gradingSD')} identifies review considerations including environmentally sensitive lands. Steep hillsides, coastal bluffs and other mapped constraints can affect the approval path. Confirm the parcel and scope before assuming ordinary building review is sufficient.</p><p>Have the project team establish whether grading review, geotechnical input or other studies are needed. Keep this step ahead of commitments to an addition footprint or a large paved area.</p>`],
['Treat drainage as a connected system','<p>Locate roof discharge, surface runoff routes, drains and any known subsurface systems. Ask where water goes now and where it will go after the project. New paving or a roof addition can change the amount and path of runoff.</p><p>A qualified civil or geotechnical professional may be needed to evaluate the site and an acceptable discharge approach. Do not redirect water toward neighboring property, a slope or a retaining wall on the assumption that moving it away from the house solves the problem.</p>'],
['Budget access and temporary work','<p>Discuss how equipment and materials reach the work area, where excavated material goes and how the existing home stays accessible. Include temporary protection and restoration in the proposal rather than assuming they are included in a foundation price.</p><p>If the design relies on retaining or supporting soil, identify the responsible designer and inspection requirements. Ask what must be completed before excavation begins and which conditions could pause the work.</p>'],
['Keep an inspection and maintenance record','<p>Photograph concealed drainage routes and retain approved plans, reports and inspection records. At handover, ask which drains and accessible components need routine attention and what changes should trigger professional review.</p><p>New or worsening movement, recurring water entry or damaged retaining structures deserve investigation. Covering the symptoms with landscaping or a new finish can make the underlying issue harder to assess.</p>']
],faq:[['Does every hillside remodel require new geotechnical work?','Not every project has the same scope. The design professional and permitting authority should establish what information is needed for the proposed work and site.'],['Can a drainage repair be kept separate from an addition?','Sometimes, but coordinate both designs first. Foundations, grading and roof runoff may share the same constrained space.']],sources:['gradingSD'],links:[['Planning an addition','addition-budget.html'],['Structural changes','structural-changes.html'],['Outdoor construction sequence','outdoor-kitchen-planning.html']]
},
{
slug:'painting-process',title:'Planning Interior Painting: Preparation, Sheen and Touch-Ups',description:'Compare painting scopes by preparation, surface repairs, primer and finish. Learn why touch-ups show and how to keep useful color and product records.',
intro:'Two painting estimates can list the same rooms and still describe very different work. The difference is often preparation: what gets repaired, which surfaces are included and what level of finish you expect to see in the room’s actual light.',
sections:[
['Define the surfaces and repairs','<p>List walls, ceilings, doors, trim and closets separately. Identify damaged areas, existing texture and any staining that needs investigation. Specify who moves and protects furniture, removes hardware and restores the room.</p><p>Do not use “prep as needed” as the only description of a wall with visible defects. Walk the room together and agree on repairs and a representative finished area. Strong side lighting can reveal surface irregularities that are less noticeable under overhead light.</p>'],
['Choose a complete coating system','<p>Record the manufacturer, product line, color and sheen for each surface. Primer and preparation should suit the substrate and existing coating. Ask the painter to explain the proposed system rather than comparing bids only by the number of finish coats.</p><p>Sheen names and performance vary by product. Review samples on the actual surface in daylight and evening light, and consider cleaning needs alongside appearance. A sample card cannot fully show how a large wall will look.</p>'],
['Plan around existing-home conditions',`<p>In a pre-1978 home, establish the lead-safe work requirements before disturbing existing painted surfaces. The ${ref('leadEPA')} explains the importance of lead-safe renovation practices. Resolve suspected moisture problems before painting over stains.</p><p>Coordinate the sequence with flooring, cabinets and trim. Agree on protection, ventilation and product-specific drying and curing instructions, especially when the home remains occupied.</p>`],
['Understand why touch-ups can show',`<p>In painting, “flashing” describes a visible difference in appearance or sheen. It is not roof flashing. Color, sheen, texture and application method can all affect how a repaired spot blends. ${ref('paintMatch')} explains why matching the original paint is only part of the task.</p><p>Test a small area and judge it after drying in the room’s light. If the patch remains visible, repainting to a natural boundary may produce a better result than repeatedly adding paint to the same spot.</p>`],
['Keep a useful finish record','<p>Label leftover paint with the room, surface, product, color formula, sheen and date. Save photographs of the labels and follow the manufacturer’s storage guidance. Record any custom matching or mixed batches.</p><p>Review the completed work in normal room lighting and list remaining defects by location. Ask when cleaning is appropriate; dry to the touch and fully cured are not interchangeable instructions.</p>']
],faq:[['Will the same color always make an invisible touch-up?','No. Sheen, aging, surface texture and application can make the spot visible even when the color formula matches.'],['Should every room use the same sheen?','No. Choose by the surface, appearance and care requirements of the specific paint system, then record the selections clearly.']],sources:['paintMatch','leadEPA'],links:[['Coordinating doors and trim','interior-doors.html'],['Protecting an occupied home','site-protection.html'],['Final walkthrough','whole-home-closeout.html']]
},
{
slug:'interior-doors',title:'Choosing Interior Doors, Trim and Finish Details Together',description:'Coordinate door construction, swing, jambs, hardware, baseboards and floor transitions. Use a finish schedule to avoid mismatched details during installation.',
intro:'A door selection is more than a panel style. The opening, swing, hardware and surrounding trim all affect whether it works comfortably and looks intentional. Make those decisions together, particularly when new work meets existing rooms.',
sections:[
['Choose construction for the room’s needs',`<p>Hollow-core, solid-core and solid-wood doors are different constructions. The ${ref('doorTypes')} is a useful starting point for comparing them. Ask the supplier about the exact product’s weight, finish options and intended use.</p><p>For a bedroom or office, discuss privacy and sound expectations. A heavier panel alone does not make a room soundproof: the frame, gaps and surrounding construction matter too. For a utility space, confirm any ventilation or other assembly requirements before changing the door.</p>`],
['Check the opening and the swing','<p>Measure the existing opening and wall thickness, and confirm whether the proposal replaces just the slab or the frame as well. Review the swing against furniture, switches, adjacent doors and bathroom fixtures.</p><p>A pocket door may save swing space, but the wall needs evaluation for structure, services and hardware access. Show the intended clear opening on the plan rather than relying only on a nominal door size.</p>'],
['Coordinate the jamb, casing and baseboard','<p>Set the relationship between casing and baseboard before ordering. Review thickness, profiles and how trim ends at cabinets, tile or built-in furniture. Existing walls may need preparation to achieve the desired result.</p><p>Check floor height changes and transitions between rooms. Ask who adjusts doors and trim after flooring is installed. A simple detail at the showroom can become awkward where an old wall is uneven or the new floor is higher.</p>'],
['Make one finish schedule for connected rooms','<p>For every opening, record the door model, size, swing, frame, casing, hardware finish and paint or stain. Add the baseboard profile and nearby floor transition. Choose one representative junction to review before repeating it throughout the house.</p><p>Matching does not require every material to be identical. For example, painted doors can work with a different cabinet finish if the trim and hardware choices are deliberate. Compare real samples together rather than trusting similar names across brands.</p>'],
['Check operation at handover','<p>Open, close and latch each door. Review clearances, alignment, hardware and visible finish repairs. Obtain care instructions and replacement hardware information, and record any adjustments still needed.</p><p>If a door’s function is part of a required fire, ventilation or accessibility arrangement, have the project professional confirm the completed assembly. Do not substitute components based on appearance alone.</p>']
],faq:[['Can I replace only the door panel?','Sometimes. The existing frame’s dimensions and condition, hinge positions and new panel compatibility need checking before ordering.'],['Should baseboards be selected after the floor?','Coordinate them together, including thickness, transitions and installation sequence. Waiting can leave awkward gaps or trim details.']],sources:['doorTypes'],links:[['Flooring choices','lvp-vs-hardwood.html'],['Paint and finish records','painting-process.html'],['Defining the scope','scope-of-work.html']]
},
{
slug:'payment-schedules',title:'California Remodel Payment Schedules and Change Orders',description:'Understand deposit limits, progress-payment milestones and written change orders. Keep the contract total, approved changes and payment records aligned.',
intro:'A payment schedule should let you see what a payment covers. A change order should let you see what changed. When either document is vague, it becomes harder to compare the project’s progress with the amount you have paid.',
sections:[
['Check the deposit rule before paying',`<p>For California home improvement contracts, CSLB states that the down payment generally cannot exceed $1,000 or 10% of the contract price, whichever is less. A rare exception applies to contractors with a qualifying blanket performance and payment bond on file with CSLB. An ordinary contractor license bond is not that exception. Verify any claimed exception through CSLB. ${ref('paymentCSLB')}.</p><p>Do not confuse the deposit with a later progress payment. Ask for each amount and its purpose in writing before transferring money.</p>`],
['Tie progress payments to identifiable work','<p>Use descriptions you can compare with the site and contract: the specified cabinets delivered, or the defined installation phase completed, rather than simply “payment three.” The milestone also needs an amount and a clear description of the work or materials it covers.</p><p>CSLB states that payments cannot exceed the value of work performed, apart from the permitted down payment. Review the proposed schedule against the contract’s scope and CSLB’s requirements. Ask what evidence supports each invoice and how partially completed milestones are handled. A calendar date alone does not explain what has been provided.</p>'],
['Approve changes before the changed work begins',`<p>Use a written change order signed by both parties before proceeding with changed work. Identify the reason, added and removed work, price adjustment and schedule effect. CSLB’s ${ref('changesCSLB')} describes the requirements.</p><p>For example, replacing a selected vanity should show the credit for the original item, the replacement cost and any plumbing or delivery effect. “Upgrade vanity” leaves too much unresolved. If the price is not yet known, establish the investigation or pricing step instead of treating an unanswered message as approval.</p>`],
['Keep one running financial record','<p>Track the original contract amount, each approved addition or credit, the revised total, payments made and the remaining balance. Keep invoices, receipts and signed changes with that record. Compare it with the contractor’s statement regularly.</p><p>An allowance reconciliation should explain what the allowance covered and how the actual selection changes that amount. Ask whether delivery, installation and taxes are already included before assuming the entire difference is an upgrade charge.</p>'],
['Resolve discrepancies while they are small','<p>If an invoice does not match the agreed milestone, ask for the supporting detail and discuss the discrepancy promptly. Keep the conversation and any resolution documented. Obtain professional advice for contract disputes or lien questions rather than relying on a generic internet rule about withholding payment.</p><p>Before closeout, reconcile approved changes and remaining work against the contract. Keep final inspection, warranty and payment records together.</p>']
],faq:[['Can a verbal upgrade agreement replace a change order?','Use a signed written change order that records scope, price and timing before the changed work proceeds. A verbal discussion is not a reliable project record.'],['Does a contractor license bond allow a larger deposit?','Not by itself. CSLB describes a separate qualifying blanket performance and payment bond exception; verify it directly if claimed.']],sources:['paymentCSLB','changesCSLB'],links:[['Allowances and contingency','allowances.html'],['Verify a contractor’s license','verify-contractor-license.html'],['Closeout checklist','whole-home-closeout.html']]
},
{
slug:'seasonal-home-checklist',title:'A Practical Home Maintenance Checklist After a San Diego Remodel',description:'Organize post-remodel checks around rain, leaks, shower care and installed equipment. Keep product instructions and clear records of changes that need attention.',
intro:'A useful maintenance checklist follows the systems in your home. Start with the records from your remodel, then build a short routine around water, exterior conditions and equipment. The goal is to notice a change before it becomes an expensive surprise.',
sections:[
['Build the checklist from the handover documents','<p>List installed equipment, finish products and accessible shutoffs. Save model numbers, warranties and the manufacturer’s care instructions. Add recommended service dates to your calendar instead of applying one generic interval to every product.</p><p>Keep a simple log with the date, location, observation and action taken. A photograph from the same position can make a developing stain or cracked joint easier to explain to a professional.</p>'],
['Check accessible exterior areas before rain','<p>From safe ground-level positions, look for blocked surface drains, debris collecting near openings and changes around doors or exterior penetrations. Arrange qualified help for roof, gutter or elevated work. Do not climb onto the roof to complete a checklist.</p><p>After rain, note recurring ponding or new water marks and have the cause evaluated. A new sealant bead is not a diagnosis of why water is entering. On a slope, changes in drainage or movement deserve particular attention.</p>'],
['Look for plumbing changes during normal use',`<p>Check accessible areas under sinks for moisture and pay attention to toilets that keep running or fixtures that develop leaks. EPA’s ${ref('waterCare')} provides a useful starting point. Have problems repaired rather than treating recurring moisture as normal.</p><p>Know where the relevant shutoffs are and ask your plumber to demonstrate them during handover. Do not operate unfamiliar equipment or disturb concealed plumbing to investigate a suspected leak.</p>`],
['Care for the shower as a system','<p>Use cleaners approved for the actual tile, stone, grout, sealant and fixture finishes. A product suitable for ceramic tile may damage natural stone or a plated finish. Avoid mixing cleaning chemicals, and follow product labels.</p><p>Use the installed ventilation as intended and keep accessible surfaces clean. Watch for damaged sealant, persistent odor or moisture outside the enclosure. Grout discoloration alone does not diagnose a waterproofing failure; recurring leakage needs investigation before cosmetic repair.</p>'],
['Review equipment and unresolved items','<p>Follow the specific service instructions for filters, fans, appliances and safety devices. Keep service access clear and arrange qualified maintenance where required. Save receipts and notes with the equipment records.</p><p>Revisit the remodel punch list and warranty terms before assuming an issue is routine wear. Report concerns with dates and photographs. A concise record is more useful than waiting until several small problems are difficult to distinguish.</p>']
],faq:[['How often should I reseal a shower?','There is no single schedule for every assembly. Distinguish stone or grout treatments from flexible sealant, and follow the installed products’ instructions and observed condition.'],['Can maintenance prevent every leak?','No. Regular observation can help identify changes, but concealed defects or failures still require qualified investigation and repair.']],sources:['waterCare'],links:[['Shower waterproofing decisions','shower-waterproofing.html'],['Tile and grout care','bathroom-tile-layout.html'],['Handover records','whole-home-closeout.html']]
},
{
slug:'wildfire-conscious-remodeling',title:'Wildfire-Conscious Remodeling for San Diego County Homes',description:'Use a remodel to review roofs, vents, exterior assemblies and maintenance access. Confirm parcel-specific fire requirements and select documented products.',
intro:'A remodel can be a practical time to address parts of a home that are difficult to reach later. Wildfire preparation needs a whole-property discussion, though: a new roof or vent does not make a house fireproof, and the surrounding conditions still matter.',
sections:[
['Confirm the requirements for the property','<p>Ask the responsible building and fire authorities which current requirements apply to the parcel and proposed alteration. San Diego County includes several jurisdictions; a mailing address alone does not identify the authority or the applicable approval path.</p><p>Have the designer separate required work from additional improvements you may choose. Record the basis for each product or assembly requirement so substitutions can be checked before ordering.</p>'],
['Review the building as a connected exterior',`<p>CAL FIRE’s ${ref('fireCAL')} addresses roofs, vents, walls, windows, decks and other vulnerable areas. Use it to organize a property review rather than choosing one highly visible improvement in isolation.</p><p>Ask the project team to explain how proposed changes meet at edges and openings. A compliant product label does not resolve every connection or installation detail. Keep the exact product documentation and installation instructions in the project file.</p>`],
['Coordinate vent changes with ventilation needs','<p>Have a qualified professional evaluate replacement vents and the required airflow. CAL FIRE specifically cautions that fire-protection modifications can affect ventilation. Do not block vents or add improvised coverings on the assumption that closing an opening is always safer.</p><p>When roof, siding or deck work is already planned, ask which related details can sensibly be addressed during that access. Price them as defined scope items, with the responsible designer and installer identified.</p>'],
['Include the area around and beneath the work','<p>Consider how a deck, fence or patio cover connects to the house and how those areas will be maintained. CAL FIRE recommends removing combustible materials beneath decks. Avoid creating inaccessible spaces that accumulate debris or become storage areas.</p><p>Confirm current defensible-space and landscaping requirements with the local fire authority. Building improvements and vegetation maintenance are separate responsibilities that should be coordinated, especially near slopes or substantial vegetation.</p>'],
['Keep documentation and an ongoing plan','<p>At completion, retain approvals, product listings, photographs of concealed details and care instructions. Ask what needs periodic inspection and how to arrange safe access. Keep emergency planning separate from the construction punch list.</p><p>No contractor should promise that these measures eliminate wildfire risk or guarantee an insurance outcome. If insurance eligibility matters to the project, discuss the proposed work and required evidence with the insurer before committing to it.</p>']
],faq:[['Does a remodel require every part of the home to be upgraded?','Requirements depend on the property, jurisdiction and scope. Ask the responsible authorities and project designer to identify what applies to this project.'],['Can I choose a material simply because it is advertised as fire-resistant?','Check the exact product, its relevant listing and its intended assembly with the project professional. A general marketing term does not establish compliance.']],sources:['fireCAL'],links:[['Coastal exposure and exterior materials','coastal-home-remodeling.html'],['Hillside and drainage considerations','hillside-properties.html'],['Post-remodel maintenance','seasonal-home-checklist.html']]
}
];
